Linux: dismiss popup windows on an outside click (xdg_popup grab)

Against flutter/flutter at b710a6e0290f7cb9c3ad55666c5f8573aed2ee8f, the
revision snap/snapcraft.yaml pins. Apply from the Flutter checkout root:

    git apply -3 /path/to/flutter-popup-grab.patch

The hunks are hand-authored against a read of that revision, so the offsets are
approximate and `git apply` may want -3 or a manual fixup. Treat it as the
change to make, not a blob to trust.

NOT APPLIED OR COMPILED ANYWHERE. This is the upstream change the
GRACEFUL_SHELL_POPUP_GRAB spike in lib/popup.dart exists to justify; do not
send it until that spike shows a `grab` request on the wire and a `popup_done`
coming back. See the spike's doc comments for what to measure.

WHY THIS IS A BUG FIX AND NOT A FEATURE

PopupWindowController's own class documentation already promises the behaviour
(_window.dart:899-903):

    /// A popup window is a transient window that is used for menus and context
    /// menus. Popups may receive input focus. When another window receives
    /// input focus, the popup is closed.

On Linux nothing implements the third sentence. PopupWindowControllerLinux
creates a GTK_WINDOW_POPUP, realizes it, sets it transient for the parent and
places it with gdk_window_move_to_rect — so GDK does map it as an xdg_popup
(should_map_as_popup returns TRUE for POSITION_METHOD_MOVE_TO_RECT) — but it
never takes a seat grab. GDK3 emits xdg_popup.grab in exactly one place, as it
creates the popup, and only when find_grab_input_seat finds a grab seat on the
popup or its transient-for chain. No grab, no request; no request, no
popup_done, and the popup stays on screen over whatever the user clicked.

THE ORDERING, WHICH IS THE ONLY SUBTLE PART

gdk_wayland_seat_grab records the seat on the window, calls the caller's
prepare_func, and then bails with a g_critical and GDK_GRAB_NOT_VIEWABLE — and
clears the seat it just recorded — if the window is still not visible:

    gdk_wayland_seat_set_grab_window (wayland_seat, native);
    ...
    if (prepare_func)
      (prepare_func) (seat, window, prepare_func_data);

    if (!gdk_window_is_visible (window))
      {
        gdk_wayland_seat_set_grab_window (wayland_seat, NULL);
        g_critical ("Window %p has not been made visible in GdkSeatGrabPrepareFunc", window);
        return GDK_GRAB_NOT_VIEWABLE;
      }

So the prepare function is where the window must be shown, and GTK's own
comment in find_grab_input_seat says as much: "this relies on GTK+ taking the
grab before showing the popup window." The map is what creates the xdg_popup
and reads the seat. Grabbing after the map is too late — GDK will not revisit
it, and xdg-shell calls it invalid_grab.

That is why the show moves *inside* the grab rather than the grab being added
beside it. The popup is still shown from onFirstFrame, so nothing about when it
becomes visible changes.

TWO THINGS A REVIEWER WILL ASK

Unconditional, or opt-in? This takes the grab for every popup, on the grounds
that it is the documented contract and that a menu which outlives the click
that dismissed it is wrong everywhere. The cost is that an explicit grab is
defined to take keyboard focus, so a popup will pull focus off whatever had it
— which is what a GTK menu does, and what most compositors do for menus, but
it is a behaviour change. If that is not acceptable, the same diff works as a
`bool grab` on PopupWindowController's factory threaded through
WindowingOwner.createPopupWindowController; nothing else changes.

Only PopupWindowControllerLinux is touched. The tooltip controller in this file
also creates a GTK_WINDOW_POPUP, and deliberately keeps no grab: a tooltip that
took the keyboard and swallowed the next click would be a worse bug than the
one this fixes.

REPORTING THE DISMISSAL

popup_done reaches the widget layer as a window state change, not a destroy:
GDK's xdg_popup_done handler calls gdk_window_hide, and gdk_window_hide
synthesizes GDK_WINDOW_STATE_WITHDRAWN for a window with an impl. So
onStateChanged plus the _GdkWindowState.withdrawn and _GdkWindow.getState()
that this file already has is the whole detection, with no new bindings.

The controller deliberately does NOT destroy itself here. destroy() calls
gtk_widget_destroy before unregistering the view, and destroying a GTK window
whose FlView still has a live view aborts the process — the destroy cascade
disposes the per-view renderer whose frame_mutex the raster thread holds, and
g_mutex_clear on a held mutex is a glib abort(). Deciding when it is safe to
destroy is the owner's job, so this adds one additive, non-breaking delegate
hook and lets the owner act on it.

